course: dictionary
lesson: 7362
title: Plead: beg, excuse, or court
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: To plead means to ask for something in a strong, emotional way, as if your whole heart is in it. It means to beg. «Mayn't I sing them just one little song?» he pleaded piteously. The word piteously is a clue: it means in a sad, sorry way that makes people feel for you, so he was begging, not just asking politely.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. «Mayn't I sing them just one little song?» he begged piteously. It still makes sense, so begged is a good match for pleaded. Check your guess by swapping in a word that fits the clues around it.
explain: Plead has other meanings too. In court, to plead guilty or not guilty means to say whether you did the crime. In a sentence like «He pleaded a headache», it means he gave a headache as his excuse. Context tells you which meaning is right: look at the words around it.
fact: Plead means to ask for something in a strong, emotional way; to beg.
fact: In court, to plead guilty or not guilty means to say whether you did the crime.
fact: To plead something, such as a headache, means to give it as an excuse.
fact: «Pleaded» is the past tense in UK English; «pled» is the past tense in US English.
fact: Special pleading means making an exception to a rule for yourself or a favourite without a real reason.
exercise: «Mayn't I sing them just one little song?» he pleaded piteously. What does pleaded mean here? | Lost his temper | Sang loudly to the crowd | *Begged in a sad, heartfelt way | Walked away quickly
exercise: In court, what does it mean to plead guilty? | *To say you did the crime | To win the case | To pay the fine straight away | To run away from the judge
exercise: «He pleaded a headache to get out of PE.» What does pleaded mean? | Shouted it loudly | *Gave it as an excuse | Forgot all about it
test: Which is the UK past tense of plead? | *pleaded | plead | pleading | pleads
test: Special pleading means... | Begging for mercy with tears | *Asking for an exception to a rule for yourself without a real reason | Following every rule carefully | Asking a judge for a fair trial
exam: In «He pleaded not guilty», what does pleaded mean? | *Said in court that he was not guilty | Shouted at the jury | Begged the judge for mercy | Ran out of the courtroom
